This file is a help2man include.  A [section] block goes in at the start of
that section and a [=section] block replaces it, while a /pattern/ block goes
in after the paragraph matching the pattern.  The note at the foot of this
file is attached to a pattern so that it lands at the end of COPYRIGHT, after
the copyright help2man makes out of --version, rather than ahead of it.
Should help2man's wording ever change, that note goes missing rather than
turning up in the wrong place.

[NAME]
buckle\-x11 \- Nostalgia bucklespring keyboard sound, X11 flavour

[=DESCRIPTION]
Buckle emulates the sound of an old IBM Model-M space saver
bucklespring keyboard while typing within an X session.
.PP
It runs as a background process and plays back the sound of each key
pressed and released on the keyboard, just as if using an IBM Model-M.
The sound of each key has carefully been sampled, and is played back
while simulating the proper distance and direction for a realistic 3D
sound palette of pure nostalgic bliss.
.PP
This flavour grabs key events through X11, and therefore only reports keys
typed within an X session.  For Wayland compositors and for the console, see
.BR buckle\-libinput (1).
Both flavours are alternatives for
.IR /usr/games/buckle ,
managed by
.BR update\-alternatives (1).
.PP
The mouse clicks too: the buttons, and the scroll wheel, one click per detent.
Use
.B \-\-mouse
to pick out only some of it, or to silence it altogether.
.PP
A key held down long enough to repeat clicks for every repeat.  The X server
does not repeat the modifier keys, so holding shift stays quiet by itself.

[SEE ALSO]
.BR buckle\-x11.service (8),
.BR buckle\-libinput (1),
.BR update\-alternatives (1)

/NO WARRANTY/
.PP
